**Async Await — 1 Minute Interview Answer**

JavaScript is single thread language.
It can do only one task at a time.

But some operations take time like API call, database call, file read.
If JavaScript waits → application becomes slow.

So JavaScript uses asynchronous programming.

First we used Promises with then and catch.
But when steps increase, code becomes complex.

So async/await introduced to solve this problem.

Async makes function return a promise.
Await waits for the result inside async function.
It pauses only that function, not whole program.

So async/await makes asynchronous code look like normal synchronous code.
It improves readability and error handling using try and catch.

In simple words:
Async/await is cleaner and better way to write promises.


Example : 
============
async function demo() {
    return "Hello";
}

demo().then(res => console.log(res));

Output:
Hello

function getData() {
    return new Promise(resolve => {
        setTimeout(() => resolve("Data received"), 2000);
    });
}

async function show() {
    const result = await getData();
    console.log(result);
}

show();
console.log("Other work running");

Output:

Other work running
Data received

So program did not stop — only function waited.

# 🔹 Error Handling in Async/Await
=======================================
We use `try...catch`

async function test() {
    try {
        const data = await getData();
        console.log(data);
    } catch (err) {
        console.log("Error:", err);
    }
}



Nice 👍 — this is **callback hell example**.
Interviewers love this question:

> “Convert callback code → Promise → Async/Await”
=====================================================


Your code means:

1. Create order
2. Do payment
3. Send SMS

But it becomes **deep nested pyramid** 😵\
---

## 1️⃣ Your Original (Callback Hell)
-----------------------------------------
    createOrder(cart, function(orderId) {
    proceedToPayment(orderId, function(paymentInfo) {
        sendSMS(paymentInfo, function(message) {
        console.log("Order complete", message);
        }, function(err) {
        console.log("SMS failed", err);
        });
    }, function(err) {
        console.log("Payment failed", err);
    });
    }, function(err) {
    console.log("Order failed", err);
    });


Problem:

* Hard to read
* Hard to debug
* Hard to maintain

---

## 2️⃣ Convert to Promise (Better)
-------------------------------------


    function createOrder(cart) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        // logic
        resolve(orderId);
    });
    }

    function proceedToPayment(orderId) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        resolve(paymentInfo);
    });
    }

    function sendSMS(paymentInfo) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        resolve("SMS Sent");
    });
    }

    createOrder(cart)
    .then(orderId => proceedToPayment(orderId))
    .then(paymentInfo => sendSMS(paymentInfo))
    .then(message => console.log("Order complete", message))
    .catch(err => console.log("Failed:", err));


Better than callback 👍
But still chaining.

---

## 3️⃣ Convert to Async/Await (Best — Industry Style)
----------------------------------------------------------------

    async function placeOrder() {
    try {
        const orderId = await createOrder(cart);
        const paymentInfo = await proceedToPayment(orderId);
        const message = await sendSMS(paymentInfo);

        console.log("Order complete", message);
    } catch (err) {
        console.log("Failed:", err);
    }
    }

    placeOrder();


---

